<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhils</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bhil, Bhil people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhils are one of the largest tribal peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, living mainly in the hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. Known historically as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and guardians of hill passes, they served in the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states, and the British raised Bhil corps in the 19th century. They speak Bhili languages and are recognised as a Scheduled Tribe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhils.jpg" alt="Bhils" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: an albumen photo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhils1880s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
